Wiring in `PlanToolbar.tsx`: `OpenThrottleToolbar`'s `utilityContent` slot already holds the 'CLI preview and history' link. Either compose both into that slot inside a flex wrapper, or add a dedicated slot to `OpenThrottleToolbar` — decide explicitly and document it in the component JSDoc; do not silently overload `utilityContent` in a way that breaks `PlanTaskToolbar`, which shares the skeleton. Add `editors` and `workingDirectory` to `PlanToolbarProps` with `@description` JSDoc matching the file's existing prop-doc style.

Thread the data in `PlanDetailRoute.tsx`: `workingDirectory` already comes out of `usePlanDetailRoute`; add the fallback to the selected checkout/repository `filesystemPath` from `loaderData.workspaceRepositories` when the atom is empty, and pass `enabledEditors` from the loader.

Rewrite `loader` in `applications/openthrottle-developer/app/routes/plans.$planId._index.tsx`, following the precedent in `app/routes/dashboard._index.tsx:52-84` (fire everything concurrently, return unawaited promises, let RR8 Single Fetch stream them).

Shape:

```ts
export const loader = async (args: Route.LoaderArgs) => {
  const { planId } = args.params;
  if (!planId) return { /* empty shape, promises resolved to empty */ };

  // Kick every request off before the first await so nothing waterfalls.
  const criticalPromise = executeGraphqlWithAuth(args.request, PlanDetailCriticalDocument, { planId });
  const enabledEditors = loadEnabledEditors(args.request);
  const ledger = executeGraphqlWithAuth(args.request, PlanDetailLedgerDocument, { planId }).then(…);
  const outputChunks = …;
  const runHistory = …;
  const tagVocabulary = …;
  const workspaceRepositories = …;

  const critical = await criticalPromise;

  return { enabledEditors, ledger, outputChunks, plan: critical.plan ?? null, runHistory, tagVocabulary, tasks: critical.tasksByPlanId ?? [], workspaceRepositories };
};
```

Rules:
- Every deferred promise must `.then()` into exactly the shape the consumer wants (arrays, defaulted to `[]`) so components never reach into query envelopes. Mirror the dashboard loader's `.then()` mapping.
- Do NOT `.catch()` the deferred promises. Rejections are the point — each region's `errorElement` handles its own failure, and a `workspaceRepositories` failure must no longer 500 the page. `loadEnabledEditors` keeps its internal catch (it already degrades to `[]`).
- The `!planId` short circuit must keep returning the same key set, with the deferred keys as already-resolved promises (`Promise.resolve([])`) so the component's boundaries stay type-uniform.
- Comment the split at the top of the loader: which key is awaited and why, and the standing rule that new fields default to deferred.

Update `app/routes/__tests__/plans.$planId._index.loader.test.ts`: it currently routes mock responses by document identity — extend `mockDocuments` to route all six documents, assert that `plan`/`tasks` come back resolved, that the deferred keys are promises, that each resolves to the mapped shape, and add a case proving a rejected `workspaceRepositories` promise does not make the loader throw.

`WORKSPACE_EDITOR_DEEP_LINKS` currently carries just `{ label, scheme }`, which cannot express the three editors' genuinely different URL shapes (see the plan description's capability table). Rework it into a per-editor descriptor plus a builder, with unit tests.

Shape to aim for (adjust naming to fit the repo):

```ts
interface PlanLaunchTarget {
  readonly buildHref: (input: { readonly planId: string; readonly workingDirectory: string }) => string | null;
  readonly label: string;
  readonly supportsFolder: boolean;
  readonly supportsPrompt: boolean;
}
```

Per-editor builders — use `URLSearchParams` for every query value (all params must be URL-encoded):
- **Claude**: `claude://code/new?` + `URLSearchParams({ folder, q: `/ot-claude-loop ${planId}` })`. The slash command must be first on line 1 of `q`.
- **Cursor**: `cursor://anysphere.cursor-deeplink/prompt?` + `URLSearchParams({ text })` where text names the skill file (`Run OpenThrottle plan <planId> following .agents/skills/ot-claude-loop/SKILL.md`). Cursor's deeplink takes **no folder param** — the prompt lands in whatever Cursor window is focused. Decide here whether Cursor ships with a tooltip stating that, or is omitted from the toolbar; record the decision in the file's JSDoc either way.
- **VS Code**: keep reusing `editorHref({ absolutePath, scheme: 'vscode' })` from `@openthrottle/react-router-ide` — open-folder only. Do not invent a prompt param; `microsoft/vscode#279701` is still open.

Return `null` from `buildHref` when the target's required input is missing (e.g. no working directory for a folder-based editor) so callers can skip the button instead of rendering a dead link.

Add co-located tests asserting: exact Claude href for a known planId + path, encoding of a path containing spaces and `#`, Cursor href has no folder, VS Code href is unchanged from today's `editorHref` output, and `null` on missing input.

Validate: `pnpm nx run openthrottle-developer:typecheck` and the new test file.

The clobber risk is the main hazard of this plan, and it is cross-feature, so it needs a test that exercises BOTH features against ONE repo rather than each in isolation.

In a single fixture repo: run foreign-skill `ensureMaterialized` and apply editor configuration, in both orders, then assert:
- `git status --porcelain` is empty;
- the injected skill entries are still excluded (i.e. foreign-skill injection did not lose its block);
- the manifest is still excluded;
- `.git/info/exclude` contains exactly two managed blocks, each with its own marker.

Then tear down the skill layer and assert only the skill block is removed while the workspace-editors block survives untouched.

This is the test that would catch a silent regression of OT b409da6e.

Owner decision before any code. The Agent Skills spec has no first-class arguments field — only `metadata` (string→string). Choose one:
(1) `metadata.openthrottle-arguments` = stringified JSON/YAML schema (spec-compatible, recommended);
(2) OT-specific top-level `arguments:` frontmatter block (cleaner authoring, superset of spec, non-portable);
(3) parse an Arguments section from the body (fragile — default no).
Also lock the per-argument schema fields (`name`, `type`, `required`, `description`, `default`, `enum`) and the invocation encoding (positional vs `--flag value`) — verify the encoding against how the harness/skill-invocation path actually consumes args, don't assume. Record the decision on the plan output before implementing.

Today a flowless episode is invisible until someone runs the recorder, and a flow whose beat labels drifted from its episode is invisible until the narration lands on the wrong picture. Both are cheap to catch mechanically and neither is caught now.

Add a flow registry beside `episodes/registry.ts` (explicit imports, same reasoning as that file: a glob is not typecheckable and turns "that flow does not exist" into a surprise at record time), and a test over it asserting:

- every registered episode has a flow **or** a non-empty `production.blockedOn` — the plan's definition of done, enforced;
- `flow.id === episode.id`, since one slug drives the module directory, the flow and the output directory;
- every `beat` label used in a flow's steps and in `regionOfInterest` names a real beat on that episode. `AGENTS.md` warns that an extra flow beat does not merely go unnarrated, it shifts every later beat's narration one beat early — that is a silent, whole-video defect and it should be a red test;
- every key of `regionOfInterest` is a beat label that some step actually uses (a region for a beat nobody labels is dead, and `run.ts` already warns about unresolved regions at record time — this catches the other direction, statically).

While here: `episodes/__tests__/selectors.test.ts` scans flows for `[data-testid="..."]` and only sees the four that exist. Confirm it picks up flows from the new registry rather than by directory walk, so a flow that lives somewhere unexpected is not silently unscanned.

Done when: deleting a flow file, renaming a beat in an episode, or pointing a region at a beat label nobody uses each fail with a message naming the episode and the label.

The fail-loud guard, relocated from seed time to export time. A committed manifest beside the exporter classifies every (table, column) in the export scope as keep (verbatim), scrub (through the identity/secret pass), or drop (nulled or omitted), and every table in the database as exported | denied | ignored — each entry with a one-line reason.

Before writing anything, the exporter reflects the live schema (information_schema.columns, pg_constraint) and compares:

- a **table not in the manifest** (new migration) fails: "table X is not classified — decide exported/denied/ignored";
- a **column not in the manifest** on an exported table fails naming table and column;
- a **manifest entry whose column no longer exists** (rename/drop) fails, reported as a rename/drop rather than a bare Postgres error;
- a **vector column whose dimension changed** since the snapshot was committed fails, so a model/dimension change cannot silently strand stale embeddings.

This manifest is deliberately the first draft of the deny list a future public read-only guest instance will need — write it as if it will be promoted.

Done when: each failure case above is reproduced in a test against a scratch database or mocked reflection, with a specific, actionable message.

